From 00a445d733086da52c6b905fa287f0ea64a85938 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 16:39:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: 5-tuple-only hash helper + sch_cake adoption

Hi all,

This RFC adds flow_hash_from_keys_small() to net/core/flow_dissector.c
as a 5-tuple-only companion to flow_hash_from_keys(), documents the
flow_keys consumer landscape, and adopts the new helper in sch_cake's
host-load accounting hashes.

Motivation
==========

Auditing nine kernel flow_keys consumers (sch_cake, sch_choke,
cls_flow, ipv4/route, ipv6/route, fib_frontend, ipv4/netfilter,
ipv6/netfilter, flow_dissector itself) shows that the canonical
5-tuple (basic.ip_proto + ports + addrs + control.addr_type) is
the highest-touch field set. The fields between them in the
flow_keys hash region (tags.flow_label, vlan, cvlan, keyid,
icmp) are read by no consumer's logic directly; they only
contribute to flow_hash_from_keys() output.

For consumers that own both endpoints of the hash comparison
(e.g. sch_cake's internal host-load counters), hashing those
"hash-only" fields is wasted cycles for zero entropy gain.

Measurements
============

Userspace microbench of siphash() on AMD Zen 2 (Threadripper PRO
3945WX): cycle cost scales in ~16 B bands (SipHash2-4 absorbs
8 B per round).

  region size   p50 cycles
  ----------- ------------
       16-32          ~80  (one harness floor band)
       40-56         ~120  (two bands)
       64-72         ~120

Full flow_hash_from_keys():
  IPv4 (40 B region): ~120 cycles p50
  IPv6 (64 B region): ~160 cycles p50

5-tuple-only hash (16 B for IPv4): ~80 cycles p50.
That is roughly a 50% reduction on IPv4.

Chi-squared distribution test (100k synthetic IPv4 5-tuples,
hash bucketed into K=256 and K=4096):
  K=256:  full=242.5  vs  small=260.6  (df=255)
  K=4096: full=4070.5 vs small=4155.3  (df=4095)

Both bucket counts land squarely in the expected uniform range
(df +/- sqrt(2*df)). The smaller region is statistically
equivalent for RSS/RFS distribution purposes — as expected
since siphash is a PRF whose distribution quality is
algorithmically independent of input length above one
absorption block.

Patches
=======

  1/3  Documentation/networking: add flow_dissector consumer
       touch matrix (NEW docs file + index.rst entry)

  2/3  net: flow_dissector: add flow_hash_from_keys_small()
       5-tuple helper (~100 LoC; new opt-in API; doesn't change
       existing call paths)

  3/3  net: sched: sch_cake: use flow_hash_from_keys_small()
       for host accounting (~8 line change; ~80 cycles saved
       per packet at the queue-management layer)

What this is NOT
================

- NOT a flow_keys struct layout change. The struct is
  ABI-affecting (BPF flow_dissector hook contract); this patch
  series only adds a new helper.
- NOT bit-exact with flow_hash_from_keys(). The new helper
  produces different hash values; callers that need the RSS
  hash must continue using the existing helper.
- NOT a sch_cake main-flow-hash change. Patch 3 switches only
  the two host_keys hashes; the cake-internal queue-index hash
  remains on flow_hash_from_keys() for minimal review surface.

Pre-post checklist done
=======================

- checkpatch.pl --strict passes (0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks)
  for all three patches.
- net/core/flow_dissector.o builds clean (gcc 15.2.0, x86_64
  defconfig).
- net/sched/sch_cake.o builds clean (gcc 15.2.0, x86_64
  defconfig).

Pre-post checklist still TODO before non-RFC posting
====================================================

- Run on a non-Zen-2 testbed (Zen 1 or Intel) to confirm the
  cycle-saving trend is microarchitecture-portable. Predicted
  yes (siphash is well-pipelined on all modern x86 cores).
- Re-run chi-squared on tuples extracted from a real packet
  capture (heavy-tailed distribution) rather than synthetic
  uniform. Predicted equivalent.
- Run any sch_cake selftest in tools/testing/selftests/.
- Render Documentation/networking/flow_dissector.rst via
  `make htmldocs` to confirm the RST renders cleanly.

This is sent as RFC for review of the helper API shape and
sch_cake adoption pattern before a final non-RFC submission.
Feedback on naming (flow_hash_from_keys_small vs alternatives),
v6 handling (currently hashes full 32 B v6 addrs; an alternative
would be a pre-hash digest), and the cover-letter content
welcome.

Thanks,
